<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durlach</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durlach is a historic town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, part of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> since 1938. It was the residence of the Protestant margraves of Baden-Durlach from 1565, who built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Karlsburg</a> palace there. French troops burned it in 1689 during the Nine Years' War, and in 1718 the court moved to the newly founded Karlsruhe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durlach.jpg" alt="Durlach" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steadfast · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_Durlach_in_Karlsruhe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
